From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: this kernel does not deliver SA_SIGINFO

Loading any content crashed the moment the EE executor took its first fault:

    # signal: 11 (SIGSEGV)          # thread name: orbis
    # reason: page fault (user read data, page not present)
    # fault address: 000000000000001a
    # rsi: 0000000000000002         # rdi: 000000089c04b900 (g_eeExecutor, valid)
    # rip: 0000000800a8bb40         -> play_libretro.prx + 0x227b40

which disassembles to the second instruction of CEeExecutor::HandleException:

    227b39:  movq 0x529eb8(%rip), %rdi    # g_eeExecutor
    227b40:  movq 0x18(%rsi), %rsi        # sigInfo->si_addr   <- rsi is 2, so 2 + 0x18 = 0x1a

So the handler ran, found its executor, and was handed a siginfo_t* of 2. rdi = 11 and rdx = a
valid stack address, with a small integer between them, is FreeBSD's ORIGINAL handler signature -
void (*)(int sig, int code, struct sigcontext *scp) - and not SA_SIGINFO's
void (*)(int, siginfo_t *, void *). The flag is set in AddExceptionHandler and does not reach the
kernel.

There is no fixing the handler, because the address it exists to read is the thing that never
arrives. DISABLE_PROTECTION is Play!'s own answer for a platform that cannot trap writes - iOS,
tvOS and 32-bit ARM builds all take it - so this takes the same one.

diff --git a/Source/ee/EeExecutor.cpp b/Source/ee/EeExecutor.cpp
index c8c72f9..0ef50e4 100644
--- a/Source/ee/EeExecutor.cpp
+++ b/Source/ee/EeExecutor.cpp
@@ -36,6 +36,25 @@
 
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+// ⚠ THIS KERNEL CALLS A SIGNAL HANDLER WITH FreeBSD'S OLD (sig, code, scp) CONVENTION, NOT
+// SA_SIGINFO'S (sig, siginfo_t*, ucontext_t*). Measured from the crash dump: SIGSEGV arrived with
+// rdi = 11 (the signal), rdx = a valid stack pointer, and rsi = 2 - a small integer where the
+// siginfo_t* belongs. HandleException's first act is `movq 0x18(%rsi), %rsi` to read si_addr, so
+// it read address 0x1a and took the process down:
+//
+//     # reason: page fault (user read data, page not present)
+//     # fault address: 000000000000001a
+//     # rsi: 0000000000000002
+//
+// The whole memory-protection scheme depends on that address, so there is nothing to salvage in
+// the handler. DISABLE_PROTECTION is Play!'s own answer for a platform that cannot trap writes -
+// it is what iOS, tvOS and 32-bit ARM builds use - and it turns AddExceptionHandler into a no-op
+// and SetMemoryProtected into a nop. The cost is that writes into already-translated code are not
+// detected by a fault, which is a correctness risk for self-modifying games rather than a crash.
+#define DISABLE_PROTECTION
+#endif
+
 static CEeExecutor* g_eeExecutor = nullptr;
 
 CEeExecutor::CEeExecutor(CMIPS& context, uint8* ram)
